  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Missouri has received $11.9B in federal contract obligations so far in FY2026 to date. Procurement activity includes 120,586 awards and an average award size of $98.3K, and obligations fell 15.2% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state, followed by the Department of Energy. Aircraft Manufacturing, Facilities Support Services, and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing are the top industries by obligations. THE BOEING COMPANY and HONEYWELL FEDERAL MANUFACTURING & TECHNOLOGIES, LLC are the largest vendors by obligations in FY2026 to date. Fiscal-year history shows variability: FY21 was $13.3B, FY22 was $11.2B, FY23 was $15.6B, FY24 was $16.2B, FY25 was $14B, and FY26 is $11.9B so far. This snapshot reflects current-year totals that are partial because figures are fiscal year to date. Focus on the largest buying agencies, the biggest vendors, and the leading NAICS industries if you need to prioritize business development or competitive analysis in Missouri's federal market.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive federal spending in Missouri?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $8.3B in Missouri in FY2026 to date. The Department of Energy obligated $2.2B, and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $413M. Other notable buyers include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $316M and the Department of Agriculture at $258M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top contractors and what industries receive the most obligations?",
        "body": "THE BOEING COMPANY received $5.3B and HONEYWELL FEDERAL MANUFACTURING & TECHNOLOGIES, LLC received $2.2B in FY2026 to date. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry at $3.5B, followed by Facilities Support Services at $2.3B and Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing at $1.3B. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers accounted for $687M and Engineering Services accounted for $362M."
      }
    ]
  },
